Dave Thomas has been consulting and programming for 50 years and still writes code almost every day.

6
00:00:31,910 --> 00:00:35,790
He's an author of The Pragmatic Programmer, one of my personal favorites, especially the 20th edition.

7
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:45,070
The Manifesto for Agile Software Development, Programming Ruby, Agile Web Development with Rails, Programming Elixir.

8
00:00:45,070 --> 00:00:47,630
and I'm not finished yet, and more recently Simplicity.

9
00:00:47,630 --> 00:00:56,430
He currently runs The Pragmatic Bookshelf, and in his spare time he is looking at better ways of. writing OO code. As in, what does that stand for?

10
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:59,910
Object-oriented. Of course.

11
00:00:59,910 --> 00:01:01,951
Dave, welcome to Software Engineering Radio. Well, thank you.

12
00:01:01,951 --> 00:01:03,550
I really appreciate being here.

13
00:01:03,550 --> 00:01:05,670
It's great to chat.

14
00:01:05,670 --> 00:01:09,751
Is there anything missed in that impressive bio of yours, or you happy with it? Well, not really.

15
00:01:09,751 --> 00:01:12,190
I mean, I I do a lot of random stuff, basically.

16
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:17,591
My wife accuses me of just doing the stuff I'm interested in, which is probably true. That's all right.

17
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:22,190
I'm a bit of a magpie when it comes to the things I'm interested in.

18
00:01:22,190 --> 00:01:26,030
so I'm never quite sure what I'm going to be attacking on a particular day or month.

19
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:27,871
That's a great way to live. I like it.

20
00:01:27,880 --> 00:01:31,910
So, before we start, I'll mention a small disclaimer.

21
00:01:31,910 --> 00:01:39,190
I'm pretty close to finishing a book I'm writing for your company, The Pragmatic Bookshelf, about Rust.

22
00:01:39,190 --> 00:01:42,270
and C, so I actually know how some of this stuff works from the author's perspective. So, I just wanted to say that.

23
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:46,110
And what are you doing talking to me and not finishing the book off, huh?

24
00:01:46,110 --> 00:01:48,070
Well, probably good point.

25
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:51,592
I was doing it this morning, don't worry. Oh, good. Good.

26
00:01:51,592 --> 00:01:53,350
Anyway, let's begin.

27
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:57,550
So, the show is called Building Ebooks Infrastructure.

28
00:01:57,550 --> 00:01:59,870
So, we need to lay the foundation.

29
00:01:59,870 --> 00:02:02,510
So, I'd like to start with an overview of what an ebook is.

30
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:10,831
So, an ebook is fundamentally just the text of a book in some kind of accessible format to. electronics.

31
00:02:10,831 --> 00:02:14,270
In the old days, which was 15,

32
00:02:14,270 --> 00:02:21,430
20 years ago, there were probably half a dozen different standards for what made an ebook.

33
00:02:21,430 --> 00:02:24,630
But nowadays, we've pretty much settled on a format called EPUB.

34
00:02:24,630 --> 00:02:28,670
And EPUB is actually I mean, if you have an EPUB file,

35
00:02:28,670 --> 00:02:31,510
you can actually just run unzip on it.

36
00:02:31,510 --> 00:02:38,110
And if you unzip an EPUB, you'll find that it's a bunch of HTML, a bunch of assets.

37
00:02:38,110 --> 00:02:40,110
and then a couple of manifest type files.

38
00:02:40,110 --> 00:02:45,430
that give it navigation and metadata and all that kind of stuff.

39
00:02:45,430 --> 00:02:52,190
So, whenever you feed an ebook into your reader, whether that's a Kindle or a Apple Books.

40
00:02:52,190 --> 00:02:56,310
or whatever it might be, all it's doing really is unzipping.

41
00:02:56,310 --> 00:03:00,710
that and then running some kind of browser clone to actually display the content.

42
00:03:00,710 --> 00:03:02,949
You mentioned there used to be other formats.

43
00:03:02,949 --> 00:03:06,509
I remember always having to send a.mobi file.

44
00:03:06,519 --> 00:03:10,670
I think you can still download those when you buy a a book from your website.

45
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:13,190
Amazon has stopped distributing mobis, I believe.

46
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:15,070
Okay, yeah.

47
00:03:15,070 --> 00:03:16,910
I may be wrong about that because now in the old days,

48
00:03:16,910 --> 00:03:22,391
you're right, we had to give Amazon a mobi file. And that was always a real pain.

49
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:30,070
because mobis were different in terms of the stuff that they would support to regular EPUBs.

50
00:03:30,070 --> 00:03:35,790
So, getting compatible files between mobis and EPUBs was always a challenge.

51
00:03:35,790 --> 00:03:41,151
But eventually, Amazon gave in and switched across. So, now we submit EPUBs to them.

52
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:45,870
And internally, they do change them into something for the Kindle.

53
00:03:45,870 --> 00:03:47,670
I don't know exactly how they do that.

54
00:03:47,670 --> 00:03:50,750
For all I know, it may well be mobis still.

55
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:53,350
But yeah, we never now see a mobi file.

56
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:58,190
And was that mainly because EPUB was a standard or is a standard and mobi wasn't?

57
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:04,310
Yeah, I think they were constantly playing catch up with the EPUB.

58
00:04:04,310 --> 00:04:09,710
and frankly it just wasn't worth it because I think initially they went mobi.

59
00:04:09,710 --> 00:04:15,030
because it gave them the ability to control stuff a bit better on the Kindle,

60
00:04:15,030 --> 00:04:19,789
but eventually EPUB does everything mobi does plus a lot more.

61
00:04:19,789 --> 00:04:23,830
It's a way more flexible format and so I think they just said,

62
00:04:23,830 --> 00:04:27,230
"Well, why are we fighting a technology war that we don't really get any benefit from?"

63
00:04:27,230 --> 00:04:29,190
So, they just switched across.

64
00:04:29,190 --> 00:04:31,710
Did they give you any help to render and produce them?

65
00:04:31,710 --> 00:04:33,631
We didn't have to do anything. We just.

66
00:04:33,631 --> 00:04:38,750
We were always generating mobis and EPUBs as part of our process.

67
00:04:38,750 --> 00:04:41,150
and so we just basically stopped sending the mobis.

68
00:04:41,150 --> 00:04:43,311
I see. And was there a big difference?

69
00:04:43,311 --> 00:04:46,630
It seems to be that they were playing catch up, you said.

70
00:04:46,630 --> 00:04:48,670
I mean, from our point of view, not at all.

71
00:04:48,670 --> 00:04:51,990
From consumer point of view, I honestly don't know.

72
00:04:51,990 --> 00:04:56,630
My guess would be over the years, yes, I think it has improved.

73
00:04:56,630 --> 00:04:59,870
So, for example, when you first started generating mobis,

74
00:04:59,870 --> 00:05:04,190
they did not support the equivalent of the pre tag,

75
00:05:04,190 --> 00:05:06,830
which is the thing that says, "Do not change the spacing."

76
00:05:06,830 --> 00:05:12,270
And that meant the code listings were always very, very challenging.

77
00:05:12,270 --> 00:05:15,150
So, for a while, we were actually converting code into images.

78
00:05:15,160 --> 00:05:21,112
and then embedding the images in the EPUB cuz it was the only way we could find to. reliably. get them to render.

79
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:25,909
There was a whole bunch of stuff like that where mobis just I mean,

80
00:05:25,909 --> 00:05:28,430
they were designed for reading fiction basically.

81
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:32,590
There was a Kindle, it's your ticket to the beach and read a book device.

82
00:05:32,590 --> 00:05:36,870
And so, anything that we needed that was a little bit out of the norm,

83
00:05:36,870 --> 00:05:41,350
we would have to fake out using a whole bunch of tables.

84
00:05:41,350 --> 00:05:43,830
or a whole bunch of images or something like that.

85
00:05:43,830 --> 00:05:46,990
And now, we don't bother.

86
00:05:46,990 --> 00:05:51,470
Now, we just use EPUB and EPUBs are gradually getting closer and closer to the browser.

87
00:05:51,470 --> 00:05:53,830
So, we get richer and richer formatting as we go forward.

88
00:05:53,840 --> 00:05:58,470
Yeah, I think I remember having to double-tap a code listing cuz it was just a big image.

89
00:05:58,470 --> 00:06:00,591
and then you'd have to zoom around and it was. Uh-huh.

90
00:06:00,591 --> 00:06:02,710
pretty painful.

91
00:06:02,710 --> 00:06:06,870
And that was painful for you as a reader and also painful for us as a publisher.

92
00:06:06,870 --> 00:06:09,750
And we were constantly bumping into various limits.

93
00:06:09,750 --> 00:06:13,550
And things like what happens if a code listing is bigger than the page.

94
00:06:13,550 --> 00:06:16,510
and that kind of thing.

95
00:06:16,510 --> 00:06:19,470
And did we get before and I guess the mobis would have been bigger in file size cuz.

96
00:06:19,470 --> 00:06:21,070
you were putting images in them rather than.

97
00:06:21,070 --> 00:06:23,510
I think they probably were, yeah.

98
00:06:23,510 --> 00:06:25,710
Initially, we started out trying to keep all of them below about 2 meg.

99
00:06:25,720 --> 00:06:29,270
because I can't remember there was some arbitrary limit someone had.

100
00:06:29,270 --> 00:06:31,070
But nowadays, that's not a problem.

101
00:06:31,070 --> 00:06:32,750
So, we again, we don't have to worry about it.

102
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:38,871
And just to clarify before we move on, is a PDF an ebook? I guess it could be.

103
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:44,990
To my mind, an ebook is something which is convenient to read electronically.

104
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,750
PDFs are certainly readable electronically.

105
00:06:48,750 --> 00:06:51,230
The question is, are they convenient?

106
00:06:51,230 --> 00:06:56,550
One of the distinguishing factors I think is whether or not it's reflowable.

107
00:06:56,550 --> 00:07:02,110
Now, that means that when I take a browser, right? If you bring a browser window up and look at a webpage,

108
00:07:02,110 --> 00:07:07,590
if you change the size of the browser window, the text grows.

109
00:07:07,590 --> 00:07:10,750
or shrinks or move expands or whatever to fill the space.

110
00:07:10,750 --> 00:07:12,430
And you don't have to paginate.

111
00:07:12,430 --> 00:07:16,790
You don't get down to the bottom of one page and then, you know,

112
00:07:16,790 --> 00:07:18,470
if you're reading an article, hit some magic key.

113
00:07:18,470 --> 00:07:23,950
and then the whole screen clears and the next page comes up.

114
00:07:23,950 --> 00:07:26,430
So, that's a kind of fixed format where and PDF is a fixed format.

115
00:07:26,430 --> 00:07:29,550
It's page-based.

116
00:07:29,550 --> 00:07:34,270
Whereas, typically, for the kind of books that we're creating and for typical fiction and everything else,

117
00:07:34,280 --> 00:07:35,790
you don't want that.

118
00:07:35,790 --> 00:07:39,430
Instead, you want it to be flow-based, which means that as I change the size of the screen.

119
00:07:39,430 --> 00:07:45,790
or I change the font, then the layout of the text adjusts to suit that.

120
00:07:45,790 --> 00:07:50,751
Would the PDF reader not do that for you, or would that just be? They kind of can now.

121
00:07:50,751 --> 00:07:56,270
You can actually tell it to turn off pagination and do that kind of reflowing.

122
00:07:56,270 --> 00:07:58,310
It messes it up more than it gets it right.

123
00:07:58,310 --> 00:08:01,630
And the reason for that is that PDF format internally,

124
00:08:01,630 --> 00:08:06,190
it doesn't really consist of a whole bunch of text with instructions around it.

125
00:08:06,190 --> 00:08:11,790
The text is broken up into very, very small chunks,

126
00:08:11,790 --> 00:08:14,550
and then position on the page as a small chunk of text.

127
00:08:14,550 --> 00:08:18,909
And so, in order to reflow it, what you've really got to do is basically analyze that text.

128
00:08:18,909 --> 00:08:22,909
and try to work out, well, this is a paragraph, because you don't know.

129
00:08:22,909 --> 00:08:28,430
It's not anything intrinsic in the PDF to say this is a paragraph.

130
00:08:28,430 --> 00:08:31,990
So, it's actually it's tricky to reflow,

131
00:08:31,990 --> 00:08:36,350
and they do it, and hats off to them for doing it, but it's not perfect.

132
00:08:36,350 --> 00:08:41,709
I did a show with Peter Wyllie and Jeff Jeff Johnson on the 30 years of PDF.

133
00:08:41,709 --> 00:08:43,630
Cuz they helped do all the spec and everything in there.

134
00:08:43,630 --> 00:08:45,430
We did a deep dive on it.

135
00:08:45,430 --> 00:08:49,270
All if listeners want to pick up show 532. Well, I'm going to go and listen to that.

136
00:08:49,270 --> 00:08:51,190
That's interesting.

137
00:08:51,190 --> 00:08:55,470
We speak about the biggest PDF in the world that covers the whole city of Japan,

138
00:08:55,470 --> 00:08:57,431
and all sorts of really cool stuff. Mhm.

139
00:08:57,440 --> 00:09:00,510
All right.

140
00:09:00,510 --> 00:09:02,190
So, I think you were hinting at this.

141
00:09:02,190 --> 00:09:04,510
but do you think books are evolving toward more like a website.

142
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:10,470
where you can get a book updated over time, either get it corrected or changed?

143
00:09:10,470 --> 00:09:12,710
Well, our books have always been like that.

144
00:09:12,710 --> 00:09:19,030
If you buy an ebook from us, then we will actually send you updates.

145
00:09:19,030 --> 00:09:23,670
And either if we find bugs, sometimes if the author does like a new revision.

146
00:09:23,670 --> 00:09:27,590
Typically not for like a second edition, because that's typically a rewrite.

147
00:09:27,590 --> 00:09:29,270
But if an author wants to do like a third or fourth printing.

148
00:09:29,270 --> 00:09:33,430
and they discover they want to add another couple of paragraphs,

149
00:09:33,430 --> 00:09:35,829
we just throw those in there and distribute it.

150
00:09:35,829 --> 00:09:41,830
And so, you just you get a notification, you download the most recent one, and you have it.

151
00:09:41,830 --> 00:09:44,430
Remember that ebooks are typically designed to be read offline.

152
00:09:44,440 --> 00:09:49,510
They may not be, but they're designed that way.

153
00:09:49,510 --> 00:09:51,750
And so we can't just magically update it in your reader.

154
00:09:51,750 --> 00:09:55,230
And in fact, you wouldn't want to. Quite often readers don't want you to do.

155
00:09:55,230 --> 00:10:00,030
that because they'll be making annotations or whatever in the existing one.

156
00:10:00,030 --> 00:10:02,630
So we make them always available.

157
00:10:02,630 --> 00:10:05,990
In a way, I think the biggest thing I'm seeing.

158
00:10:05,990 --> 00:10:13,310
though is that we're getting more adventurous in what we can do inside a book.

159
00:10:13,310 --> 00:10:15,190
So it's actually not many people know this,

160
00:10:15,190 --> 00:10:19,990
but you know you have disclosure lists in HTML where you can have a heading.

161
00:10:19,990 --> 00:10:25,270
and you click on it and then the text below it expands.

162
00:10:25,270 --> 00:10:27,431
and you get a whole bunch of backup material for that. Yeah, like a.

163
00:10:27,431 --> 00:10:31,110
I think in the JavaScript sort of widget, well, they call them accordions, don't they?

164
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:34,711
Accordions, yeah, but in fact that's built into HTML. Oh, okay.

165
00:10:34,711 --> 00:10:40,230
It's doesn't have to have any JavaScript running at all.

166
00:10:40,230 --> 00:10:43,430
And a lot of ebook readers actually support that.

167
00:10:43,430 --> 00:10:48,870
And way back when we actually experimented with putting that into our ebooks.

168
00:10:48,870 --> 00:10:53,190
So you could actually have like a summary list which if you clicked on a particular link,

169
00:10:53,190 --> 00:10:56,990
that would expand to show you the detail.

170
00:10:56,990 --> 00:10:58,550
And at the time we ran into the problem that.

171
00:10:58,550 --> 00:11:04,350
because most ebook readers back then were e-ink displays,

172
00:11:04,350 --> 00:11:09,030
then it basically had to clear the screen and redraw it with the expanded text.

173
00:11:09,030 --> 00:11:12,190
And people just found that too distracting. But that's an example of the kind of thing.

174
00:11:12,190 --> 00:11:18,550
that you can do in an ebook that you can't do in most other book style output,

175
00:11:18,550 --> 00:11:20,671
and certainly. not in paper.

176
00:11:20,671 --> 00:11:23,630
Yeah, it sounds like you could enrich the page more.

177
00:11:23,630 --> 00:11:28,951
and keep relevant text but not immediately present it. Yeah, absolutely.

178
00:11:28,951 --> 00:11:35,190
And we kind of do that with footnotes now in our ePubs.

179
00:11:35,190 --> 00:11:37,510
A footnote is a link and you tap the link and it goes to the footnote.

180
00:11:37,520 --> 00:11:40,350
and then on the footnote you tap another link.

181
00:11:40,350 --> 00:11:42,390
and it takes you back to where you were in the text.

182
00:11:42,390 --> 00:11:44,470
And yeah, that kind of thing is definitely possible.

183
00:11:44,470 --> 00:11:46,870
One of the things that we have discovered.

184
00:11:46,870 --> 00:11:51,910
though is that one of the attributes of a book is.

185
00:11:51,910 --> 00:11:55,470
that it is kind of what's the word?

186
00:11:55,470 --> 00:11:59,750
Boring in terms of the way it's laid out and everything else.

187
00:11:59,750 --> 00:12:02,230
You know what to expect when you have a book.

188
00:12:02,230 --> 00:12:05,030
And so if you were reading a book.

189
00:12:05,030 --> 00:12:09,030
and it suddenly went all psychedelic on you or all the fonts changed or something,

190
00:12:09,030 --> 00:12:12,270
that would be really distracting.

191
00:12:12,270 --> 00:12:14,190
And a book is not supposed to be a distraction.

192
00:12:14,190 --> 00:12:20,310
A book is simply a substrate where an author can get content to a reader.

193
00:12:20,310 --> 00:12:23,190
And once you start making it all gee whiz,

194
00:12:23,190 --> 00:12:27,950
look at the technology, you can kind of get in the way of that communication.

195
00:12:27,950 --> 00:12:33,951
That breaks my heart because I love experimenting with the kind of wild stuff you can do inside. an e-pub.

196
00:12:33,951 --> 00:12:38,910
but it's just too risky to be honest with you to do that.

197
00:12:38,910 --> 00:12:41,470
People will complain if you do that.

198
00:12:41,470 --> 00:12:45,350
Yeah, it's a sort of medium to disappear into, isn't it? And. Exactly right.

199
00:12:45,360 --> 00:12:50,031
and no distractions, unlike the rest of the world. Exactly.

200
00:12:50,031 --> 00:12:54,671
Yeah, I mean the last thing you want is a pop-up ad in an e-book. I think they would try if they.

201
00:12:54,680 --> 00:12:56,470
Oh, I mean you can do it.

202
00:12:56,470 --> 00:13:01,311
You can actually do it, but yeah, that would be disastrous. Cool.

203
00:13:01,311 --> 00:13:05,870
So, I'm going to take us on to um middle part of our chat today.

204
00:13:05,870 --> 00:13:10,950
So, now that we know what an e-book is and the formats that you support as a publisher.

205
00:13:10,950 --> 00:13:15,991
and pretty much everyone supports, which is e-pub, like you said, let's talk about building them. Okay.

206
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,710
So, building e-pubs.

207
00:13:18,720 --> 00:13:24,552
First question, what do authors write books in? Word? No.

208
00:13:24,552 --> 00:13:26,190
And that's actually, you know what?

209
00:13:26,190 --> 00:13:30,390
It may be easier to do this slightly differently and go back and look at the history.

210
00:13:30,390 --> 00:13:34,430
Go because that actually informs a lot of what we do.

211
00:13:34,440 --> 00:13:42,750
So, the whole book business started in 1997, I guess,

212
00:13:42,750 --> 00:13:44,990
when Andy Hunt and I started putting together The Pragmatic Programmer.

213
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,270
Except we weren't doing The Pragmatic Programmer at the time.

214
00:13:48,270 --> 00:13:53,670
We were just doing a set of notes that we could give our clients.

215
00:13:53,670 --> 00:13:55,390
because we would turn up at a client,

216
00:13:55,390 --> 00:13:57,550
we were doing consulting at the time, we'd turn up at a client.

217
00:13:57,550 --> 00:14:04,790
and we'd always spend the first couple of days saying the same things to every client.

218
00:14:04,790 --> 00:14:10,030
Stuff like, maybe you want to test that before you ship it, or can you build this automatically.

219
00:14:10,030 --> 00:14:14,950
and all that kind of pretty obvious stuff, but at the time it wasn't obvious.

220
00:14:14,950 --> 00:14:19,190
So, we put together some notes and they were just HTML pages.

221
00:14:19,190 --> 00:14:21,910
that we were going to print out.

222
00:14:21,910 --> 00:14:24,230
And it was actually my wife who said,

223
00:14:24,230 --> 00:14:30,110
"Why don't you see if you can get a publisher in this is Nexus good stuff."

224
00:14:30,110 --> 00:14:33,470
And so, we thought, "Well, no, there's no way people would be interested in that as a book."

225
00:14:33,470 --> 00:14:35,790
But, we came up with this cunning plan,

226
00:14:35,790 --> 00:14:37,829
which was we would submit it to.

227
00:14:37,829 --> 00:14:42,550
who we thought was the best publisher for this kind of material.

228
00:14:42,550 --> 00:14:44,550
and they would reject it, but when they rejected it,

229
00:14:44,550 --> 00:14:49,070
they would say why they rejected it and that would help us to make it better.

230
00:14:49,070 --> 00:14:54,470
So, we submitted it to Addison-Wesley and they screwed us over by accepting it.

231
00:14:54,470 --> 00:14:56,870
And so, we were stuck with having to produce this book.

232
00:14:56,880 --> 00:15:00,550
And we already had a whole bunch of content.

233
00:15:00,550 --> 00:15:07,710
Andy and I uh really strong believers in the power of plain text.

234
00:15:07,710 --> 00:15:10,350
So, text that has basically just words, just characters.

235
00:15:10,350 --> 00:15:16,790
So, we'd never ever used word processors to do anything.

236
00:15:16,790 --> 00:15:20,030
Back then, 1997-98, that was basically Microsoft Word.

237
00:15:20,030 --> 00:15:26,590
And Word has got better, but Word does not produce the kind of quality layout.

238
00:15:26,590 --> 00:15:29,990
that you need for a book.

239
00:15:29,990 --> 00:15:32,110
So, back then, if you were an author,

240
00:15:32,110 --> 00:15:36,630
the chances were pretty good you would be authoring in Microsoft Word,

241
00:15:36,630 --> 00:15:38,510
and you would submit your book to your publisher.

242
00:15:38,520 --> 00:15:45,750
And the publisher would take your Word document, and they would import it into their own layout software.

243
00:15:45,750 --> 00:15:47,990
And that might be something like FrameMaker.

244
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,350
Nowadays, it would be InDesign.

245
00:15:50,350 --> 00:15:52,030
Whatever it might be.

246
00:15:52,030 --> 00:15:54,550
And that's a one-way process.

247
00:15:54,550 --> 00:15:56,750
They ingest it, it gets into their tool,

248
00:15:56,750 --> 00:16:02,190
and at that point, the master copy of the book is in their hands.

249
00:16:02,190 --> 00:16:04,670
and they do whatever they have to do to make it look good.

250
00:16:04,670 --> 00:16:10,151
So, now I'm an author, and I have discovered an error in the book, and I want to fix it.

251
00:16:10,151 --> 00:16:14,670
Well, I cannot fix the Word document anymore, because that's now irrelevant.

252
00:16:14,670 --> 00:16:16,350
It's not anything to do with the book anymore.

253
00:16:16,360 --> 00:16:18,390
So, what I have to do is to.

254
00:16:18,390 --> 00:16:26,110
basically send my changes to the publisher as a set of "On page 27,

255
00:16:26,110 --> 00:16:28,750
line four, change the word this to that."

256
00:16:28,750 --> 00:16:32,590
And then, some poor person at the other end has to go through,

257
00:16:32,590 --> 00:16:38,030
read those instructions, bring up the FrameMaker, and make the whatever the adjustment is.

258
00:16:38,030 --> 00:16:41,350
And to us, that just felt like this impossible, we're never going to do that.

259
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:49,710
So, we convinced Addison-Wesley to let us give them a camera-ready PDF.

260
00:16:49,720 --> 00:16:53,350
Actually, it was a PostScript file at the time.

261
00:16:53,350 --> 00:16:56,950
And we asked them for their spec, and they gave us their spec for typesetting.

262
00:16:56,950 --> 00:17:02,470
And then, we set about putting together a tool chain that would let us write plain text,

263
00:17:02,470 --> 00:17:04,790
and have it end up being a book.

264
00:17:04,790 --> 00:17:06,870
And we went through a couple of iterations.

265
00:17:06,870 --> 00:17:09,829
The first one, we used a tool called troff, t r o f f.

266
00:17:09,839 --> 00:17:14,110
That's t r o f f.

267
00:17:14,110 --> 00:17:17,470
And troff was, at that point, easily 20 years old.

268
00:17:17,470 --> 00:17:24,030
It was part of the Unix document as workbench that came out of Bell Labs.

269
00:17:24,030 --> 00:17:28,189
And in troff, what you did is you basically typed your text.

270
00:17:28,189 --> 00:17:30,950
and then you inserted various formatting instructions by.

271
00:17:30,950 --> 00:17:36,191
basically you would start a line with a period a full stop. and then you would put like a one.

272
00:17:36,200 --> 00:17:44,190
or two character instruction like this is a title or this is a page break or something.

273
00:17:44,190 --> 00:17:49,270
And it would then take your text and format it using those instructions. Kind of like HTML almost with your title.

274
00:17:49,280 --> 00:17:54,190
Kind of like HTML way more restricted and way more specialized.

275
00:17:54,190 --> 00:17:59,070
But the thing that made it usable is that it had a macro system.

276
00:17:59,070 --> 00:18:02,310
So you could define commands using the lower level commands.

277
00:18:02,320 --> 00:18:07,710
So we produced this library that would do the layout and it kind of worked.

278
00:18:07,710 --> 00:18:10,150
But troff was written for academic papers.

279
00:18:10,160 --> 00:18:12,510
It wasn't written for book type stuff.

280
00:18:12,520 --> 00:18:16,070
And a lot of people have written the books in troff.

281
00:18:16,070 --> 00:18:18,710
but honestly it doesn't do a very good job of paragraph breaking.

282
00:18:18,720 --> 00:18:23,030
where it decides where to put a new line in a paragraph.

283
00:18:23,030 --> 00:18:27,311
It just looks a bit ugly. So we switched across to using TeX.

284
00:18:27,320 --> 00:18:34,830
which was Don Knuth's layout system which makes way way nicer output.

285
00:18:34,830 --> 00:18:41,150
And so we produced a set or I produced a set of LaTeX macros to define the book.

286
00:18:41,150 --> 00:18:42,830
And we wrote the book in LaTeX.

287
00:18:42,840 --> 00:18:49,190
and we delivered the PDFs to Addison-Wesley and to our surprise they just said, "Yep, that's good."

288
00:18:49,190 --> 00:18:53,110
So the first edition of Pragmatic Programmer was produced that way.

289
00:18:53,110 --> 00:18:58,311
But the nice thing was I think the first edition I can't remember how many printings we had. It was a lot.

290
00:18:58,311 --> 00:19:03,110
And they would come to us and say, "Any changes you want to make?"

291
00:19:03,110 --> 00:19:05,590
And we actually had the ability just to go through the book.

292
00:19:05,590 --> 00:19:09,830
and update stuff and then regenerate the PDF and send it to them.

293
00:19:09,830 --> 00:19:11,870
It was kind of interesting at the time.

294
00:19:11,870 --> 00:19:17,310
What happened was you would send them the PDF, they would run it through something called a rip.

295
00:19:17,310 --> 00:19:20,270
which converts it into a high resolution set of images,

296
00:19:20,270 --> 00:19:25,070
and then they would burn those images onto metal plates.

297
00:19:25,070 --> 00:19:27,990
and those metal plates would then go into the printing presses,

298
00:19:27,990 --> 00:19:33,470
and you've probably seen the things on TV, these massive big machines with big drums that are spinning.

299
00:19:33,470 --> 00:19:37,390
and paper flies through, goes in white, comes out with text on it.

300
00:19:37,390 --> 00:19:42,350
So, there's actually a decent cost associated with converting a PDF into those metal plates.

301
00:19:42,350 --> 00:19:47,150
So, what they tried to do is to reuse plates that haven't changed.

302
00:19:47,150 --> 00:19:48,950
And so, we had to come up with a system.

303
00:19:48,950 --> 00:19:55,831
where we could automatically tell them which pages had changed and which pages hadn't to reduce their costs. But, that was fun.

304
00:19:55,831 --> 00:19:57,990
And that was groundbreaking for that time.

305
00:19:57,990 --> 00:19:59,791
I don't know, maybe. I don't know.

306
00:19:59,791 --> 00:20:02,350
Here's the thing, we didn't know what we were doing.

307
00:20:02,360 --> 00:20:06,910
We were just doing what sounded like a good idea at the time.

308
00:20:06,910 --> 00:20:11,630
And so, we were experimenting, and we just doing what we thought was a good idea.

309
00:20:11,630 --> 00:20:16,390
So, thanks for the history of how you got started in text.

310
00:20:16,390 --> 00:20:20,110
So, that answers our question if things are written in Word or not.

311
00:20:20,110 --> 00:20:22,310
I guess other publishers might write in Word.

312
00:20:22,310 --> 00:20:25,470
In technical publishing, that used to be the case.

313
00:20:25,470 --> 00:20:28,590
Nowadays, some don't.

314
00:20:28,590 --> 00:20:31,150
Some still do take Word, but Word is just seems like such a crazy thing.

315
00:20:31,160 --> 00:20:34,870
It's really not suited for writing books.

316
00:20:34,870 --> 00:20:40,190
Added to which, if you've ever tried writing a 500-page Word document, it doesn't like that at all.

317
00:20:40,190 --> 00:20:42,990
All right, it bogs down, and it's pretty ugly.

318
00:20:42,990 --> 00:20:45,870
So, when you write a book for The Pragmatic Programmer,

319
00:20:45,880 --> 00:20:50,430
does an author use like a what you see is what you get editor.

320
00:20:50,430 --> 00:20:53,151
or what what is the actual text format? No.

321
00:20:53,151 --> 00:21:01,550
So, in the intermediate days, we switched from using LaTeX to using an XML-based markup.

322
00:21:01,550 --> 00:21:04,390
Basically, it was a simplified HTML.

323
00:21:04,390 --> 00:21:10,070
And so, you would write in this HTML thing, we called it PML for pragmatic markup language.

324
00:21:10,070 --> 00:21:13,110
And it was semantic markup.

325
00:21:13,110 --> 00:21:18,510
So, you would have tags for here's some code, here's a sidebar, whatever it might be.

326
00:21:18,510 --> 00:21:20,670
And that's what you would write.

327
00:21:20,670 --> 00:21:24,910
And authors had the ability then to be able to run our tool chain on their local computer.

328
00:21:24,910 --> 00:21:28,630
and generate either a PDF or an EPUB.

329
00:21:28,630 --> 00:21:31,310
And they could then look at their output that way.

330
00:21:31,320 --> 00:21:36,310
So, the answer is no, they don't write in a WYSIWYG format.

331
00:21:36,310 --> 00:21:38,350
And there's two reasons for that.

332
00:21:38,350 --> 00:21:42,190
First of all, I always like there's a quote from Brian Kernighan who said,

333
00:21:42,190 --> 00:21:44,830
"WYSIWYG really should be WYSIWAG.

334
00:21:44,830 --> 00:21:47,110
It's what you see is all you get."

335
00:21:47,110 --> 00:21:49,710
So, there's no additional semantic information in there.

336
00:21:49,710 --> 00:21:54,950
And that's we wanted to have that extra content.

337
00:21:54,950 --> 00:22:00,390
But secondly, if you give an author a tool that lets them generate a WYSIWYG tool,

338
00:22:00,390 --> 00:22:02,350
then what they tend to do is focus on that layout.

339
00:22:02,350 --> 00:22:07,790
And they'll spend a long time twiddling with margins and fonts and everything else.

340
00:22:07,790 --> 00:22:09,830
And that's not what we want.

341
00:22:09,830 --> 00:22:14,510
What we want is authors to focus on their content because we have people to do the layout.

342
00:22:14,520 --> 00:22:22,191
And so, it's actually I think a benefit not to be able to see the actual laid out. text as you're typing it.

343
00:22:22,191 --> 00:22:27,670
So, in the old days, we used to have an XML based layout called PML.

344
00:22:27,670 --> 00:22:32,270
And authors would write in this PML and then the tool chain would convert that into,

345
00:22:32,270 --> 00:22:34,990
you know, PDFs or ebooks or whatever.

346
00:22:34,990 --> 00:22:42,031
We have moved across to using markdown as our input. And it's a work in progress.

347
00:22:42,031 --> 00:22:49,030
We started off by allowing people to embed markdown inside their PML documents.

348
00:22:49,030 --> 00:22:59,630
And in the last year or so, we now encourage authors to write in.md files, markdown files.

349
00:22:59,630 --> 00:23:05,390
And you can still use your old PML tags inside that markdown if you want to,

350
00:23:05,390 --> 00:23:09,550
but we're basically slowly moving to the point where you no longer have to.

351
00:23:09,550 --> 00:23:13,750
And so, we actually have a markdown that's rich enough to let you express everything you need to.

352
00:23:13,750 --> 00:23:15,750
do, not just as an author of a book.

353
00:23:15,750 --> 00:23:17,670
but also as a publisher of a book.

354
00:23:17,670 --> 00:23:23,951
because there's lots of little small subtle things we have to do in the background to control the. process of it.

355
00:23:23,951 --> 00:23:27,590
So, has markdown evolved since you created PML?

356
00:23:27,590 --> 00:23:31,190
Markdown didn't even exist when we started creating PML, I don't think.

357
00:23:31,190 --> 00:23:36,350
Back then it was Textile was the equivalent of markdown.

358
00:23:36,350 --> 00:23:41,790
So, yeah, markdown has, well, first of all, it's become the de facto plain text text writing language,

359
00:23:41,790 --> 00:23:49,430
but also over the years the practicality of markdown has expanded.

360
00:23:49,430 --> 00:23:54,030
And, for example, the ability to embed HTML inside markdown in a kind of rational way means.

361
00:23:54,040 --> 00:23:58,071
that you don't have to be limited by just a few things. that markdown can do.

362
00:23:58,080 --> 00:24:08,590
And a consensus is being gradually reached on how you write extensions for markdown.

363
00:24:08,590 --> 00:24:13,470
And so that we can add extra facilities to our markdown in a kind of consistent way.

364
00:24:13,470 --> 00:24:15,790
So, yeah, markdown has matured a lot.

365
00:24:15,790 --> 00:24:18,150
And, like I say, it's what I write all my books in now.

366
00:24:18,150 --> 00:24:22,791
I want to ask a few technical questions about how you handle markdown then. Sure.

367
00:24:22,791 --> 00:24:24,550
How errors.

368
00:24:24,550 --> 00:24:26,590
So, you've got your own tool chain.

369
00:24:26,590 --> 00:24:28,750
that you've written that I don't know.

370
00:24:28,750 --> 00:24:30,791
if it's still bits of it from when you originally did it and. Oh, yeah.

371
00:24:30,800 --> 00:24:34,230
Yeah.

372
00:24:34,230 --> 00:24:37,952
So, how do you handle errors in that raw format? Mhm. As well as we can.

373
00:24:37,960 --> 00:24:42,430
So, let me talk you a little bit how the tool chain actually works.

374
00:24:42,430 --> 00:24:45,390
So, what we have is a pipeline.

375
00:24:45,390 --> 00:24:48,990
So, at one end of it you have a set of markdown files.

376
00:24:48,990 --> 00:24:53,030
And one of those markdown files will be called book.md.

377
00:24:53,040 --> 00:25:01,550
And it will typically include all of the different markdown files that make up your book.

378
00:25:01,550 --> 00:25:04,630
We encourage authors to do at least a separate chapter per file.

379
00:25:04,630 --> 00:25:11,510
Some authors actually have each section as a file or whatever, it doesn't really matter.

380
00:25:11,510 --> 00:25:13,950
So, you feed that markdown top-level file into a pipeline.

381
00:25:13,950 --> 00:25:18,110
and the pipeline goes through it's a variable number depending on the author.

382
00:25:18,110 --> 00:25:23,430
and the book, but at least half a dozen different preprocessors.

383
00:25:23,430 --> 00:25:29,430
The first one is the one that goes through and takes that book.pml and it goes through.

384
00:25:29,430 --> 00:25:34,990
and looks for all these inclusions and replaces them with the actual contents of the files.

385
00:25:34,990 --> 00:25:40,030
And that produces a stream of text that doesn't no longer has includes in it.

386
00:25:40,040 --> 00:25:46,310
That stream gets passed to the next thing and the next thing and the next thing.

387
00:25:46,310 --> 00:25:52,910
At some point in that process, we convert the markdown into XML and not coincidentally,

388
00:25:52,910 --> 00:25:58,950
the XML we convert it into is actually our original PML format.

389
00:25:58,950 --> 00:26:05,590
And the reason for that is that we have tens of thousands of lines of code.

390
00:26:05,590 --> 00:26:09,870
that know how to handle that PML format to generate books in PDF, mobi.

391
00:26:09,880 --> 00:26:13,070
epub, whatever formats.

392
00:26:13,070 --> 00:26:15,710
And so by using that as our intermediate target,

393
00:26:15,710 --> 00:26:20,110
we can reuse a whole bunch of our tooling as we go forward.

394
00:26:20,110 --> 00:26:22,310
It also means that we can do a more gradual switch over to markdown.

395
00:26:22,320 --> 00:26:28,110
because authors can still embed PML markup in their markdown documents.

396
00:26:28,110 --> 00:26:36,151
and that just makes it all the way through the various stages in the pipeline until it gets. to the PML converter.

397
00:26:36,151 --> 00:26:43,470
So, if we're targeting an ebook, what we have to do is convert your PML into XHTML,

398
00:26:43,480 --> 00:26:46,510
a 1999 standard.

399
00:26:46,510 --> 00:26:49,550
If we're converting it to a PDF,

400
00:26:49,550 --> 00:26:51,270
then what we actually do is we convert it in a couple of steps.

401
00:26:51,270 --> 00:26:53,590
which we'll talk about in a minute.

402
00:26:53,590 --> 00:27:01,190
And to do that conversion from one format of XML to HTML,

403
00:27:01,190 --> 00:27:03,110
we use a fantastic technology called XSLT.

404
00:27:03,120 --> 00:27:13,510
which is a way of specifying a set of transforms that you apply to an XML document,

405
00:27:13,510 --> 00:27:16,190
which sounds terribly terribly technical.

406
00:27:16,190 --> 00:27:18,790
But, all it really means is you give it a set of rules.

407
00:27:18,790 --> 00:27:21,630
You say to it, "Whenever you see a code tag,

408
00:27:21,630 --> 00:27:31,190
for example, replace it with a pre and then whatever the content of that code tag is."

409
00:27:31,190 --> 00:27:35,550
And so, you actually write this very functional style set of templates.

410
00:27:35,560 --> 00:27:41,470
that translate your XML into whatever format you want.

411
00:27:41,470 --> 00:27:44,631
So, for the ebooks, that is purely. That's all we do.

412
00:27:44,631 --> 00:27:48,270
We have some XSLT that takes the input document.

413
00:27:48,280 --> 00:27:56,751
and generates literally all of the files that are necessary to create, say, an epub. So, we're generating each of the.

414
00:27:56,760 --> 00:27:58,430
It's not even chapters.

415
00:27:58,430 --> 00:28:01,030
We have to break the book down into chunks.

416
00:28:01,030 --> 00:28:08,270
because epub readers need each section to be no longer than, I don't know, three or four pages.

417
00:28:08,270 --> 00:28:10,310
You can't have like the entire book in a single chunk.

418
00:28:10,310 --> 00:28:14,110
So, we break it into chunks, we set them out to separate files,

419
00:28:14,110 --> 00:28:16,110
we do all the cross-referencing, we generate the manifests.

420
00:28:16,110 --> 00:28:18,710
All of that stuff is done in XSL.

421
00:28:18,710 --> 00:28:23,710
The only additional code that we have is the code that normalizes our images.

422
00:28:23,710 --> 00:28:27,390
Some ebook readers insist that you only give them black and white images, for example,

423
00:28:27,390 --> 00:28:29,030
so we have to convert those.

424
00:28:29,040 --> 00:28:36,910
If we are generating PDFs, we go through a similar path but with a different outcome.

425
00:28:36,910 --> 00:28:42,150
We still use XSLT, but then what we do is we generate another XML format,

426
00:28:42,150 --> 00:28:44,790
which is called XSL:FO.

427
00:28:44,800 --> 00:28:52,790
And the FO stands for formatting objects.

428
00:28:52,790 --> 00:28:57,710
And FO is frankly, I'm amazed it works because what it takes on is incredibly complicated.

429
00:28:57,710 --> 00:29:04,430
It's basically a way of describing text laid out in two dimensions.

430
00:29:04,430 --> 00:29:06,910
and is also a way of describing the structure of a book.

431
00:29:06,910 --> 00:29:12,950
so that the book effectively is a container for the text that it's creating.

432
00:29:12,950 --> 00:29:18,270
It is still to me a miracle that it actually works,

433
00:29:18,270 --> 00:29:20,790
but it does and it produces gorgeous looking PDFs.

434
00:29:20,800 --> 00:29:25,990
So that is the essence of the tool chain.

435
00:29:25,990 --> 00:29:29,830
It runs, like I say, as a pipeline.

436
00:29:29,830 --> 00:29:32,790
and the pipeline varies per book because different authors have different things they want to be able to.

437
00:29:32,800 --> 00:29:35,550
do.

438
00:29:35,550 --> 00:29:40,150
So for example, some authors want to be able to include mathematical equations in their books.

439
00:29:40,150 --> 00:29:42,590
And we have a preprocessor that will do that.

440
00:29:42,590 --> 00:29:44,190
But if you don't have math in your book,

441
00:29:44,200 --> 00:29:48,390
there's no point in slowing down the build by including that preprocessor.

442
00:29:48,390 --> 00:29:50,430
So those kind of things are optional.

443
00:29:50,430 --> 00:29:55,030
So in total, we probably have, oh, I don't know, 20 different preprocessors that people.

444
00:29:55,040 --> 00:30:01,070
something the author needs to pick to pre process or is that detected at the start?

445
00:30:01,070 --> 00:30:02,870
When you sign up, you get to check out your repository.

446
00:30:02,870 --> 00:30:08,950
and that's preloaded with the ones that just about everybody uses.

447
00:30:08,950 --> 00:30:10,630
And then your editor will say, you know,

448
00:30:10,630 --> 00:30:15,230
you'll say to the editor, "Gosh, I really wish I could do an equation here." And they'll go, "Oh, okay, that's easy."

449
00:30:15,230 --> 00:30:19,270
And I'll show you how to do that by adding whatever the preprocessor is to make it happen.

450
00:30:19,270 --> 00:30:20,990
I see.

451
00:30:20,990 --> 00:30:24,270
And if it does, getting back to the question, was how are errors handled? I guess.

452
00:30:24,280 --> 00:30:25,791
Oh, that's right. I'm sorry.

453
00:30:25,791 --> 00:30:27,470
You're absolutely right.

454
00:30:27,470 --> 00:30:32,310
So the problem we have there is it's exactly the same as a compiler.

455
00:30:32,310 --> 00:30:34,150
or something and that is by the time you're sort of six.

456
00:30:34,150 --> 00:30:39,990
or seven levels deep in preprocessors, you've lost all of the original information about.

457
00:30:39,990 --> 00:30:42,950
where you were in the input document.

458
00:30:42,950 --> 00:30:48,310
And so, what we do, and it's not perfect, but when the includer runs.

459
00:30:48,310 --> 00:30:55,311
the first thing it does is it adds XML comments to the stream every time it includes a. file.

460
00:30:55,320 --> 00:30:59,750
and that includes the file name and a bit of other information.

461
00:30:59,760 --> 00:31:08,110
And then every preprocessor that changes the text as it goes through will flag the start.

462
00:31:08,110 --> 00:31:11,070
and end of that change with another comment to say,

463
00:31:11,070 --> 00:31:15,390
"I am at the relative line 27 into the current chunk.".

464
00:31:15,400 --> 00:31:20,190
And so, at some point, if you get an error.

465
00:31:20,190 --> 00:31:23,230
what it then does is it goes back through.

466
00:31:23,230 --> 00:31:27,870
that kind of stack of location things to work out where you actually were in the original document.

467
00:31:27,870 --> 00:31:30,910
And it tries to report it.

468
00:31:30,910 --> 00:31:36,790
Typically, it can get it within a few lines, unless you're looking at a really.

469
00:31:36,790 --> 00:31:42,350
really long run of text, in which case you may be off like a dozen lines.

470
00:31:42,350 --> 00:31:46,070
because there's none of these little flags to try to record where you were in the original.

471
00:31:46,070 --> 00:31:51,990
So, it's definitely a problem, and it's something I'm trying to working on refining always,

472
00:31:51,990 --> 00:31:54,150
but it's getting better than it was.

473
00:31:54,150 --> 00:31:58,110
And you've already answered one of my questions, which was going to be about equations.

474
00:31:58,110 --> 00:32:04,231
so they'll be enabled at the start manually whether you're going to do LaTeX or something like that. Right.

475
00:32:04,231 --> 00:32:06,990
How are links and references handled within the book?

476
00:32:06,990 --> 00:32:11,510
So, that is actually an ongoing question because if you think about it,

477
00:32:11,510 --> 00:32:17,310
if you're in a PDF, then imagine you're just reading on paper.

478
00:32:17,310 --> 00:32:19,190
You know, there's no hyperlinks.

479
00:32:19,190 --> 00:32:21,590
You can't press on the paper and it turns the page for you.

480
00:32:21,590 --> 00:32:25,950
So, when you do a cross reference in a paper book, what you have to do is say.

481
00:32:25,950 --> 00:32:29,350
"As you can see in the section on,

482
00:32:29,350 --> 00:32:33,190
you know, editing a file on page 27, you do this.".

483
00:32:33,200 --> 00:32:41,950
So, a cross reference there is basically a verbose description of where you're pointing to.

484
00:32:41,950 --> 00:32:44,510
If it's a ebook, then the same thing can just be a hyperlink,

485
00:32:44,510 --> 00:32:46,390
and you click on it and it takes you there.

486
00:32:46,400 --> 00:32:54,591
And what we wanted was single a single markup that would let you express both of those. And we're very close.

487
00:32:54,591 --> 00:32:59,150
There's some cases where it's not going to work, but 99% of the time.

488
00:32:59,150 --> 00:33:01,510
what you do is you say, "Whenever you have a destination,

489
00:33:01,520 --> 00:33:07,230
somewhere you want to point to, you tag it.

490
00:33:07,230 --> 00:33:09,430
You give it an ID, and then in the body of the book,

491
00:33:09,430 --> 00:33:13,390
you can say, 'Cross-reference to this target.'

492
00:33:13,390 --> 00:33:17,750
And if it is an ebook, it will generate a hyperlink for that.

493
00:33:17,750 --> 00:33:19,750
If it's a paper book, it will go.

494
00:33:19,750 --> 00:33:22,950
and have a look at that target and find a good name for it."

495
00:33:22,950 --> 00:33:27,350
So, if it's a section, it will say the section named and then the title.

496
00:33:27,350 --> 00:33:31,870
If it's a diagram, it will give you the figure number.

497
00:33:31,870 --> 00:33:33,430
If it's a line of code, it will actually give you,

498
00:33:33,430 --> 00:33:36,950
you know, line 27 of the code or whatever.

499
00:33:36,950 --> 00:33:42,750
And so, it will expand your cross-reference into a textual description,

500
00:33:42,750 --> 00:33:45,030
just as if it was a real paper book.

501
00:33:45,030 --> 00:33:49,790
And you already mentioned, I think, how people can handle code embedding or formatting.

502
00:33:49,790 --> 00:33:55,430
So, code would be a code tag in your PML, you said.

503
00:33:55,430 --> 00:33:58,550
Yeah, in PML it's a code tag.

504
00:33:58,550 --> 00:34:04,470
In Markdown, it's just a Markdown fenced block, and you put the language after the fence. Must be backtick things, isn't it?

505
00:34:04,480 --> 00:34:06,830
In the backticks or tildes, yeah.

506
00:34:06,830 --> 00:34:11,190
And so, it will take that and it will do syntax highlighting on it.

507
00:34:11,190 --> 00:34:19,270
and then generate whatever is necessary to format that either for the ebook or for the PDF.

508
00:34:19,270 --> 00:34:21,030
The PDF typically, we syntax highlight to black and white,

509
00:34:21,030 --> 00:34:24,909
but we actually also have books that are in color.

510
00:34:24,909 --> 00:34:30,230
printed in color, in which case the syntax highlighting survives as, you know, different colors.

511
00:34:30,230 --> 00:34:39,430
But we also have markup inside the code, where our tool chain is aware of all different languages.

512
00:34:39,430 --> 00:34:43,869
and it knows about what constitutes a comment in each of those different languages.

513
00:34:43,869 --> 00:34:48,310
So, if you see for example, it would be a block with a slash star.

514
00:34:48,310 --> 00:34:50,750
and then some text and star slash.

515
00:34:50,750 --> 00:34:53,190
If it was Ruby, it would be a pound character, a hash character.

516
00:34:53,190 --> 00:34:59,430
And it looks for markup inside comments to make it do things.

517
00:34:59,430 --> 00:35:03,990
So, that's where I can do things and say, "This is a destination.

518
00:35:03,990 --> 00:35:05,950
I want to put an ID on this line of code."

519
00:35:05,950 --> 00:35:10,630
And then in the body of the text, you can say, "As you can see on".

520
00:35:10,630 --> 00:35:16,150
and then reference that ID and it will say line 27 or whatever of the code.

521
00:35:16,150 --> 00:35:20,550
It's also how we do highlighting of or calling out particular lines of code.

522
00:35:20,550 --> 00:35:25,510
So, quite often authors want to show which lines changed as they're running through an example.

523
00:35:25,510 --> 00:35:31,310
And so, we have this markup where you can say, "Start emphasizing this code." And then "end."

524
00:35:31,310 --> 00:35:33,590
And it will actually put little arrows in the margin to show you that.

525
00:35:33,600 --> 00:35:39,110
And then finally, the really important thing is in the old days,

526
00:35:39,110 --> 00:35:43,030
what you would do is you would take the code that you wanted to describe.

527
00:35:43,030 --> 00:35:46,030
and then cut and paste it into the inline text of the book.

528
00:35:46,030 --> 00:35:51,070
And the problem with that is that if you then went through and updated the code,

529
00:35:51,080 --> 00:35:55,110
how would you know that you'd correctly updated everything in the book as well?

530
00:35:55,120 --> 00:36:03,231
Because a particular chunk of code might occur on six different pages in the book as you're building. up an example, say.

531
00:36:03,240 --> 00:36:11,031
So, what we wanted to do is to encourage authors not to put code inline in the text. of the book.

532
00:36:11,031 --> 00:36:14,550
but to keep code as code in program source files.

533
00:36:14,550 --> 00:36:16,390
And one of the reasons we want them to do.

534
00:36:16,390 --> 00:36:21,670
that is so that they can actually test that code as part of the book building process.

535
00:36:21,680 --> 00:36:23,990
So, for example, in my Ruby book,

536
00:36:23,990 --> 00:36:30,950
every single example of code in that book is actually run every time the book is built.

537
00:36:30,950 --> 00:36:34,190
and it verifies that the output hasn't changed.

538
00:36:34,190 --> 00:36:36,830
So, that if for example, they make some change to Ruby.

539
00:36:36,830 --> 00:36:40,990
and one of my examples no longer works, the book won't build.

540
00:36:40,990 --> 00:36:42,830
and it'll tell me that that's that's changed.

541
00:36:42,830 --> 00:36:48,270
So, to make that work, you typically don't want every code example to have all of the code.

542
00:36:48,280 --> 00:36:53,270
you just want it to highlight one particular section as you're talking about it.

543
00:36:53,270 --> 00:36:59,070
So, one of the markups we can have in code is the ability to define regions of.

544
00:36:59,070 --> 00:37:01,150
that code, sections of that code, and give them a name.

545
00:37:01,150 --> 00:37:03,150
And then when you include the code in your book,

546
00:37:03,160 --> 00:37:08,110
you say include from this file the sections named this, this.

547
00:37:08,110 --> 00:37:11,230
and this and it will just include those lines into the book.

548
00:37:11,240 --> 00:37:15,070
You mentioned already that our authors can build the book locally,

549
00:37:15,070 --> 00:37:19,510
so I guess the the whole tool chain is fairly smallish.

550
00:37:19,510 --> 00:37:22,951
and you can get all set up locally. It can be.

551
00:37:22,951 --> 00:37:26,950
I was just wondering about your sort of the integration testing there of your own code.

552
00:37:26,950 --> 00:37:28,670
Is that something extra you need to do locally?

553
00:37:28,680 --> 00:37:32,590
Yeah, that takes a little bit of work, but it's not onerous.

554
00:37:32,590 --> 00:37:37,870
Like all of our editors, for example, well, most of our editors are English majors.

555
00:37:37,870 --> 00:37:41,270
and most of them are set up to be able to build their on their own machines.

556
00:37:41,280 --> 00:37:44,470
One of the joys of my life is.

557
00:37:44,470 --> 00:37:52,750
when an editor whose prior taste of computing was using Microsoft Windows. and Word gets online and says, "You know,

558
00:37:52,750 --> 00:37:55,870
well, I tried to set that environment variable and it didn't work.".

559
00:37:55,870 --> 00:37:58,070
You know, it's kind of fun just.

560
00:37:58,070 --> 00:38:02,430
that they can they're I'm corrupting a whole generation of editors there.

561
00:38:02,430 --> 00:38:06,870
But, yeah, setting the tool chain up typically involves you have a version control tool.

562
00:38:06,870 --> 00:38:09,350
We currently use Subversion for historical reasons.

563
00:38:09,350 --> 00:38:11,590
So, you would check out your repository.

564
00:38:11,590 --> 00:38:15,190
and that gives you a whole bunch of stuff which is kind of like the boilerplate.

565
00:38:15,200 --> 00:38:19,310
but it gives you all of our tool chain as well.

566
00:38:19,310 --> 00:38:26,710
And so, given the repository, all you have to do typically is install a version of Java.

567
00:38:26,710 --> 00:38:32,230
and that's because tools that do our XSL-FO processing are Java based.

568
00:38:32,230 --> 00:38:36,790
So, we took the advantage of having Java lying around.

569
00:38:36,790 --> 00:38:39,390
and everything else we've got is based off that Java runtime.

570
00:38:39,390 --> 00:38:43,590
The tools are not written in Java, they're written in Ruby,

571
00:38:43,590 --> 00:38:46,550
but we run JRuby, which is a version of Ruby that runs inside the JVM.

572
00:38:46,560 --> 00:38:51,190
and that lets us basically you have one dependency,

573
00:38:51,190 --> 00:38:53,990
which is this Java and then the rest just typically kind of works.

574
00:38:54,000 --> 00:38:59,470
So, you obviously write your own tools for this because there was nothing before.

575
00:38:59,470 --> 00:39:01,430
Authors can build everything locally.

576
00:39:01,430 --> 00:39:06,030
If you want and you can put a bit more work in and do all your code examples.

577
00:39:06,030 --> 00:39:09,470
and just for our listeners and myself,

578
00:39:09,470 --> 00:39:11,910
I have all my Rust and C code in individual Git repository.

579
00:39:11,910 --> 00:39:14,030
So, every time I make a change to code,

580
00:39:14,030 --> 00:39:18,071
I commit that and then commit it into the repository as well. Right.

581
00:39:18,071 --> 00:39:25,110
Actually, some of our authors also have an automated set of cherry-pick tools that they use with that.

582
00:39:25,110 --> 00:39:30,190
So, they'll have a Git repo, which represents each of the steps in an example they're building.

583
00:39:30,190 --> 00:39:33,590
and if they discover they need to make a change to one of the early steps,

584
00:39:33,590 --> 00:39:35,390
they make that change.

585
00:39:35,390 --> 00:39:40,710
and then they can make it ripple through all the later changes automatically, which is kind of cool.

586
00:39:40,720 --> 00:39:45,190
So, I'm presuming your distributors for the book.

587
00:39:45,190 --> 00:39:46,870
and partners will also have a copy of this tool chain.

588
00:39:46,870 --> 00:39:48,830
or do they get a slightly different one?

589
00:39:48,830 --> 00:39:51,750
No, we send them the result.

590
00:39:51,750 --> 00:39:54,150
So, as an author when you build,

591
00:39:54,150 --> 00:39:58,190
you get a kind of lightweight copy of the PDF or the ePub.

592
00:39:58,190 --> 00:40:00,870
When it's time for production, we take your book.

593
00:40:00,880 --> 00:40:06,310
and we move it across to a protected section of the repository.

594
00:40:06,310 --> 00:40:11,310
and at that point we get to use the kind of production version of the tools,

595
00:40:11,310 --> 00:40:16,510
which to be honest with you are about 90% the same.

596
00:40:16,510 --> 00:40:19,630
The difference is that we don't resample images, we normalize stuff,

597
00:40:19,630 --> 00:40:24,110
but we also make sure that all the color has been removed.

598
00:40:24,110 --> 00:40:26,070
and we convert everything to black and white.

599
00:40:26,070 --> 00:40:27,590
which to be honest with you, if you.

600
00:40:27,590 --> 00:40:31,790
actually had a look at one of the PDFs we send off to the printer.

601
00:40:31,790 --> 00:40:33,871
you would probably wrinkle your nose at it. They look pretty ugly.

602
00:40:33,880 --> 00:40:38,070
They're a lot darker than you'd expect, for example,

603
00:40:38,070 --> 00:40:40,510
and that's simply because of the way the printing process works.

604
00:40:40,520 --> 00:40:45,590
It's taken us a long time to fine-tune that,

605
00:40:45,590 --> 00:40:47,550
but you have to manipulate things quite a bit to get it to work.

606
00:40:47,550 --> 00:40:49,190
And also, we have to send it off at a way,

607
00:40:49,190 --> 00:40:54,430
way higher resolution than we use for screen reading.

608
00:40:54,430 --> 00:40:57,670
Do you use any, or have any plans to use any AI tools, for example.

609
00:40:57,680 --> 00:41:07,910
for things like fact-checking, locating references for unreferenced content, checking that external references are properly used, i.e. links.

610
00:41:07,910 --> 00:41:13,990
or assessing content, you know, in the editing process for things like structure and clarity?

611
00:41:13,990 --> 00:41:15,590
So, yeah, I mean, the first, whatever,

612
00:41:15,590 --> 00:41:18,750
up until the thing where you said assessing content, we actually do that already,

613
00:41:18,750 --> 00:41:21,430
and we've done that for a long time.

614
00:41:21,430 --> 00:41:27,790
As part of the production process, we actually go through and validate all external links.

615
00:41:27,790 --> 00:41:32,030
and as part of any build, we actually validate all the internal links,

616
00:41:32,030 --> 00:41:35,110
and authors will get told if their links are invalid.

617
00:41:35,110 --> 00:41:40,950
We also validate things like, since the accessibility is important.

618
00:41:40,950 --> 00:41:46,950
we do things like make sure that all of your images have alt tags to describe them, and this kind of stuff.

619
00:41:46,950 --> 00:41:52,430
So, all that kind of stuff is done, but on the AI front, here's the thing.

620
00:41:52,430 --> 00:41:56,430
We have a rule that you cannot use AI to write the content.

621
00:41:56,440 --> 00:42:03,470
So, as an author, feel free to use AI as a research tool,

622
00:42:03,470 --> 00:42:05,390
or as a sounding board, or as whatever you want.

623
00:42:05,390 --> 00:42:11,030
but we actually run AI checkers on the books that were given by an author,

624
00:42:11,030 --> 00:42:17,190
just to make sure that it does not have AI generated content.

625
00:42:17,190 --> 00:42:24,471
And the reason for that is that we think the value we bring in the AI age is. that our books don't hallucinate.

626
00:42:24,480 --> 00:42:29,910
All right, our books are the result of a human being giving it their best.

627
00:42:29,910 --> 00:42:36,310
And so we don't want our authors to be using AI to generate content.

628
00:42:36,310 --> 00:42:41,470
Now, at the same time, we do have something we we released fairly recently,

629
00:42:41,470 --> 00:42:46,550
a couple months ago, called the Pragmatic Assistant.

630
00:42:46,550 --> 00:42:53,550
And that is a ChatGPT project that has been trained on probably about 40 separate pieces of documents.

631
00:42:53,550 --> 00:42:59,750
and memos and all sorts of stuff about what it means to be a bookshelf book.

632
00:42:59,750 --> 00:43:03,550
So we you know, it has all of our writing style guidelines,

633
00:43:03,550 --> 00:43:05,830
mockup information, all that kind of stuff.

634
00:43:05,830 --> 00:43:11,270
And we encourage authors to use that, particularly when they're starting out,

635
00:43:11,270 --> 00:43:16,230
because we have a style, we have a voice that we like people to use.

636
00:43:16,230 --> 00:43:21,830
And if you've never written long-form before, it can be quite tricky to get that right.

637
00:43:21,830 --> 00:43:24,950
And so in the old days, that used to be what the development editor would do.

638
00:43:24,950 --> 00:43:27,910
You would submit your book and the development editor would go,

639
00:43:27,910 --> 00:43:29,710
"Oh no, no, no, no, I like that."

640
00:43:29,710 --> 00:43:32,590
Now you can basically cut and paste a chapter or a whole chapter or a paragraph.

641
00:43:32,590 --> 00:43:36,630
or something into the AI and it'll come back and say,

642
00:43:36,630 --> 00:43:38,470
"You really shouldn't use the passive voice here.

643
00:43:38,470 --> 00:43:40,110
That should be second person, not first person."

644
00:43:40,110 --> 00:43:47,791
Or actually, it would probably just say, you know, "Don't use I, use we or you here." And this kind of stuff.

645
00:43:47,791 --> 00:43:51,190
And so we're still refining that, but we're finding authors are quite liking.

646
00:43:51,190 --> 00:43:56,750
that as a kind of angel on their shoulder kind of AI.

647
00:43:56,750 --> 00:44:01,231
Yeah, I've used that myself when I had to prepare the audience statement for. Oh yeah, yeah.

648
00:44:01,231 --> 00:44:05,190
the first round of submission.

649
00:44:05,190 --> 00:44:09,190
Opened up the ChatGPT Pragmatic Program Agent, I think it was chatbot or something.

650
00:44:09,190 --> 00:44:13,511
Pasted what I'd written and it went, "Oh, no, no, no, no, no." Yeah.

651
00:44:13,520 --> 00:44:15,310
"I know how to do this."

652
00:44:15,310 --> 00:44:18,190
And he rolled up his sleeves and went, "No, this is how it should look."

653
00:44:18,190 --> 00:44:20,150
And I was like, "But that doesn't sound like me."

654
00:44:20,150 --> 00:44:21,670
Yeah, and that's the thing is it's not like,

655
00:44:21,680 --> 00:44:26,230
you know, we're not handing control over to an AI.

656
00:44:26,230 --> 00:44:30,671
It's purely I mean, I use it all the time as a kind of I don't know about. you.

657
00:44:30,671 --> 00:44:33,310
but I always find I get my best ideas by describing them out loud.

658
00:44:33,320 --> 00:44:34,990
Yeah, the rubber ducky.

659
00:44:34,990 --> 00:44:38,270
The rubber ducking, right.

660
00:44:38,270 --> 00:44:40,190
And an AI is a fantastic rubber duck.

661
00:44:40,190 --> 00:44:45,310
One of the cool things we discovered about this is, you know that chat GPT, right?

662
00:44:45,310 --> 00:44:49,511
It always starts off by saying, "What a wonderful human being you are." Right?

663
00:44:49,511 --> 00:44:52,550
"That was really, really good, Dave."

